{"description": "\nThe scenario involves daily temperature readings in a city over a year with a heatwave event occurring in the middle of summer. The heatwave is signaled by a sudden rise in temperature for a week. The time series is sampled at a daily rate over a year, producing 365 readings.\n", "description_short": "\nDaily temperature readings over a year in a city experiencing a mid-summer heatwave.\n", "description_tiny": "\nCity temperature readings.\n", "characteristics": "\n1. Overall, there  should be a sinusoidal pattern reflecting the annual temperature cycle with highs in the summer months and lows during the winter.\n2. We can expect a sudden and sustained increase in temperature during the mid-summer heatwave event.\n3. The heatwave event might shift the distribution of temperature readings, skewing them towards higher values for the summer.\n4. Post-heatwave, we should observe a gradual return to normal temperature patterns, reflecting the end of the heatwave.\n5. Despite the heatwave event, the overall yearly pattern of temperatures increasing gradually through spring, peaking in summer, and then decreasing through autumn and winter should be preserved.\n", "generator": "\n```python\nimport numpy as np\ndef generate_series():\n    time = np.arange(365)\n    baseline = 20 + 10 * np.sin(2 * np.pi * time / 365)\n    heatwave = np.zeros_like(time)\n    heatwave[180:187] = 10 # adding a heatwave\n    noise = np.random.normal(0, 2, 365)\n    temperature = baseline + heatwave + noise\n    return temperature\n```\n", "metadata": {"start": "01-01-2022", "end": "31-12-2022", "units": "Degrees Celsius", "frequency": "Daily"}, "series": [18.408518466437087, 19.351446203039085, 20.413052834057282, 19.47155632683461, 23.956181708398553, 23.128448946819738, 23.441547617172322, 20.691938548357093, 21.304035652968572, 22.723933480851656, 21.906398664595947, 23.558184816377945, 20.401292417352963, 19.849591571639316, 23.330512869299312, 21.347965132900857, 20.630808