{"description": " A professional marathon trainer records her daily heart rate while jogging in preparation for an upcoming national marathon. External factors like weather conditions and health could alter her normal heart rate readings. The sample rate is once per day over a year amounting to 365 observations. ", "description_short": " Daily heart rate of a professional marathon trainer over a year with possible influences from weather and health conditions. ", "description_tiny": " Marathon trainer's heart rate. ", "characteristics": "\n1. The series will generally show a consistent pattern, reflecting the runner's stable workout routine.\n2. Increased heart rate during warmer summer months due to increased physical exertion.\n3. Lower heart rate during rest days or when the runner is facing health issues.\n4. A possible sudden significant drop in the heart rate towards the end of the time period might indicate the time when she participates in the marathon and the recovery period thereafter.\n5. Spikes in heart rate possible due to increased training intensity or periods of stress.\n", "generator": "\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndef generate_series():\n    np.random.seed(0)\n    baseline = 60\n    daily_fluctuation = np.random.normal(0,1,365)\n    seasonal_effect = 10*np.sin(np.linspace(0,2*np.pi,365))\n    health_event = np.random.normal(-10,3,(365//5))\n    health_event = np.concatenate((health_event, np.zeros(365 - len(health_event))))\n    np.random.shuffle(health_event)\n    marathon_effect = np.concatenate((np.zeros(335), -20*np.ones(30)))\n    heart_rate = baseline + daily_fluctuation + seasonal_effect + health_event + marathon_effect\n    heart_rate = np.where(heart_rate<0,baseline/2,heart_rate) # ensuring heart rate never drops to zero or negative\n    return heart_rate\n```\n", "metadata": {"start": "2022-01-01", "end": "2022-12-31", "units": "beats per minute", "frequency": "daily"}, "series": [61.76405234596766, 60.572763617